        <section id="chapter-1">
            <h2>1. Overview</h2>
            <p><strong>DaB 2D Quantity Takeoff</strong> is a custom AutoCAD and Civil 3D utility designed to streamline extraction and calculation of linear lengths, surface areas, and item counts directly from your CAD drawings.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="chapter-2">
            <h2>2. Core Operation</h2>
            <p>Upon activating the plugin via the command line (<code>QTY_UTI</code>), users are greeted with a primary workflow choice. The application operates in two main modes: <strong>Quick</strong> and <strong>Advanced</strong>.</p>
            <p>Once calculations are processed, results are populated cleanly into a dedicated result window with options to review and export dataset metrics.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="chapter-3">
            <h2>3. Quick Option</h2>
            <p>The <strong>Quick</strong> option is designed for rapid, automated evaluation. When selected, the tool instantly scans the drawing environment and compiles all linear, area, and count quantities across defined criteria without requiring complex manual setup.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="chapter-4">
            <h2>4. Advanced Option</h2>
            <p>The <strong>Advanced</strong> option grants full user control over filtering, categorization, and custom measurements.</p>
            
            <h3 id="ui-layout">4.1 Advanced UI Layout</h3>
            <p>The Advanced interface presents a split layout: the left pane displays all available drawing layers, while the central workspace features separate tabs for <strong>Linear</strong>, <strong>Area</strong>, and <strong>Count</strong> metrics accompanied by <strong>Add</strong> and <strong>Remove</strong> control buttons.</p>

            <h3 id="source-display">4.2 Source & Display Columns</h3>
            <p>Inside each tab, items are organized into two columns: <strong>Source</strong> and <strong>Display</strong>. Note that items assigned the exact same display name will automatically aggregate and add their quantities together into a single summary line.</p>

            <h3 id="item-addition">4.3 Item Addition Methods</h3>
            <p>Items can be added to your takeoff lists through multiple workflows:</p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>By Layer:</strong> Pull elements directly based on source layer assignments.</li>
                <li><strong>From Selection:</strong> Manually pick specific entities directly from model space.</li>
                <li><strong>Manual Tracing:</strong> If no pre-existing geometry is available, users can define quantities manually by tracing either a linear path or a closed area boundary directly in the drawing.</li>
            </ul>

            <h3 id="calculation">4.4 Calculation</h3>
            <p>Once your sources and display categories are fully configured and mapped, click the <strong>Calculate</strong> button to process the rules against the current drawing data.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="chapter-5">
            <h2>5. Result Window</h2>
            <p>The final <strong>Result Window</strong> displays a structured table of all extracted quantities, categorized neatly by type. From here, users can review totals and export data to standard external formats for reporting and cost estimation.</p>
        </section>
